  "wave": "Implement Wave Motion in React / Next.js with TypeScript and Motion for React.\n\nPurpose: A traveling wave lifts cards with gentle phase offsets.\n\nMotion behavior and states:\nTYPE:\nSequential / Wave\n\nCards form a horizontal composition.\n\nCreate a smooth wave traveling through the items.\n\nExample:\n\ncard 1 rises\n\u2192 card 2 rises\n\u2192 card 3 rises\n\u2192 card 4 rises\n\u2192 card 5 rises\n\nPrevious cards simultaneously settle.\n\nUse phase/timing offsets.\n\nCombine subtle:\n\ntranslateY\nrotate\nscale\n\nAvoid cartoon-like bouncing.\n\nImplementation: Use motion.div, variants, and animate. Use linear timing for seamless continuous movement.\nKeep animation transforms on wrappers so existing transforms and handlers are preserved. Expose typed configuration for items, active index, motion intensity, and timing.\n\nInteraction: Autoplay, swipe, or arrow keys. Provide named previous/next buttons where navigation applies, keyboard focus and arrow controls, and touch equivalents for pointer behavior.\n\nResponsive behavior: Measure the available container; reduce travel, depth, and rotation on narrow screens without changing existing content dimensions.\n\nPerformance: Prefer transform and opacity, pause offscreen work with IntersectionObserver, suspend autoplay on hidden tabs, and clean up observers, listeners, and animation frames on unmount.\n\nAccessibility: Respect prefers-reduced-motion with a visible static state and immediate navigation; provide pause controls for autoplay, visible focus, meaningful labels, and appropriate semantics. Hidden faces must not remain keyboard-focusable.\n\nReusability: Accept arbitrary existing React children, stable item keys, and optional callbacks; do not couple motion to demo card appearance.\n\nApply this motion system to the specified existing elements while preserving their current content, dimensions, typography, colors, styling, responsive behavior and functionality. Do not redesign or restructure the existing UI unless required for the animation wrapper.",
  "cube": "Implement Cube Rotation in React / Next.js with TypeScript and Motion for React.\n\nPurpose: Rotate actual faces around a virtual three-dimensional structure.\n\nMotion behavior and states:\nTYPE:\n3D Structural\n\nCards should behave like surfaces positioned around a virtual 3D structure.\n\nNavigation rotates the composition around the Y-axis.\n\nUse:\n\nperspective\nrotateY\ntranslateZ\npreserve-3d\n\nThe next surface should naturally rotate into the front position.\n\nAvoid faking the effect using only opacity.\n\nImplementation: Use motion.div, variants, and animate. Start with a spring of stiffness 180, damping 24, mass 0.8; tune to the element size.\nKeep animation transforms on wrappers so existing transforms and handlers are preserved. Expose typed configuration for items, active index, motion intensity, and timing.\n\nInteraction: Autoplay, swipe, or arrow keys. Provide named previous/next buttons where navigation applies, keyboard focus and arrow controls, and touch equivalents for pointer behavior.\n\nResponsive behavior: Measure the available container; reduce travel, depth, and rotation on narrow screens without changing existing content dimensions.\n\nPerformance: Prefer transform and opacity, pause offscreen work with IntersectionObserver, suspend autoplay on hidden tabs, and clean up observers, listeners, and animation frames on unmount.\n\nAccessibility: Respect prefers-reduced-motion with a visible static state and immediate navigation; provide pause controls for autoplay, visible focus, meaningful labels, and appropriate semantics. Hidden faces must not remain keyboard-focusable.\n\nReusability: Accept arbitrary existing React children, stable item keys, and optional callbacks; do not couple motion to demo card appearance.\n\nApply this motion system to the specified existing elements while preserving their current content, dimensions, typography, colors, styling, responsive behavior and functionality. Do not redesign or restructure the existing UI unless required for the animation wrapper.",
